|  Law guardians can and do seek drastic remedies when they feel unable
|  to control how a protective mother deals with her children. Even social
|  workers who deal with such families under the auspices of a court order
|  take it on themselves to decide what subjects a mother should discuss
|  with her child during visits, or that she should not whisper - or even how
|  often she should tell the child she loves her. (Examples of such conduct
|  have been discussed in Part II of this book.)Thus, in today’s family court system, the doctrine of parens patriae
.  has taken on proportions that seriously threaten a more basic legal principle: the limitations on state power carved out by the Bill of Rights


